PLATFORM-CAPABILITY EVIDENCE (BUILD_EXIT/TEST_EXIT root cause — NOT a code defect)

The WinUI 3 XAML markup compiler shipped with the pinned WindowsAppSDK 2.1.0
(Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Markup.Compiler) cannot run against the .NET 10 SDKs installed on this
runner. Every Release build of TeslaSync.App fails at the XAML compile target with:
    Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Markup.Compiler.interop.targets(847,9): Xaml Internal Error error
    WMC9999: Object reference not set to an instance of an object.
  cascading to WMC0001/WMC0110 "Unknown type 'TsButton'/'TsBadge'/..." for the app's own types.

Root-cause isolation performed (all independent of this change):
  1. Baseline (pre-change, clean tree) build FAILS identically — the new widget did not introduce it.
  2. Out-of-proc compiler (default UseXamlCompilerExecutable=true) runs the net472 XamlCompiler.exe:
       - standalone it loads fine (prints usage, exit 0);
       - on the build's generated obj\...\input.json it exits 1 and writes output.json with an EMPTY
         "GeneratedCodeFiles":[] (it cannot resolve the project types from the .NET 10 reference
         assemblies), so the OutputDeserializer task dereferences null -> WMC9999.
  3. In-proc compiler (UseXamlCompilerExecutable=false) loads tools\net6.0\Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Markup.Compiler.dll
       and fails: MSB4061 "the CompileXaml task could not be instantiated ... Type must be a type provided
       by the runtime" — the net6.0 task will not instantiate under the .NET 10 MSBuild.
  4. Tried .NET SDK 10.0.300 (global) AND 10.0.100 (GA, installed side-by-side) — identical WMC9999.
  5. Tried explicit -p:Platform=x64 and a full obj/bin clean — identical WMC9999.
  6. VS 2022 Enterprise MSBuild is bound to .NET SDK 9.0.314 and cannot target net10.0 (NETSDK1045),
       so the full MSBuild toolchain is not an alternative here.
  7. The committed green sibling gate logs (e.g. 0028-ClimateStatusWidget: BUILD_EXIT=0, 2500 headless
       tests pass) were produced in a DIFFERENT clone (D:\repos\teslasync-wt\wt-04), confirming this is a
       per-runner WinUI-toolchain capability gap, not a repository/code regression.

Per the artifact capability clause ("requires WinUI 3 (Windows App SDK 1.6+) + .NET 10 ... If unavailable
on the runner -> STATUS=BLOCKED with API evidence"), the solution-level build/test cannot reach 0 here.

